With overlap, the end of one chunk is repeated at the beginning of the next chunk.
This gives retrieval another opportunity to capture an idea that lies near a chunk boundary.

TASK 4 - CHUNK SIZE AND OVERLAP JUSTIFICATION
======================================================================

Chosen configuration:
- Chunk size: 400 tokens
- Overlap: 40 tokens

Why 400 tokens?
A 400-token chunk is large enough to preserve a meaningful piece of a clinical document while remaining small enough for efficient retrieval.

Why 40-token overlap?
A 40-token overlap represents 10% of the chunk. It provides boundary context without duplicating too much text.

Cost and retrieval trade-off:
Increasing chunk size reduces the number of chunks but places more text into each retrieval result.
Increasing overlap preserves more boundary context but creates duplicated tokens, increasing embedding and storage costs.

These values are a starting point for PharmaLens. They should later be evaluated using retrieval precision, recall, answer correctness, and context-window constraints.

